WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.967
It worked in the demo.

2
00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.556
Everyone is impressed.

3
00:00:03.556 --> 00:00:08.067
You have to raise concerns without becoming the person who blocks everything.

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:14.567
A colleague demos a new prompting approach that improved accuracy by forty percent.

5
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:16.313
But look at the evidence.

6
00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:17.933
Twelve hand picked examples.

7
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:20.633
Graded by the person who wrote the prompt.

8
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:23.800
And compared against the old prompt at a different temperature.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.100
Rank the flaws by how much they matter.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.633
Twelve examples is far too few to separate a real gain from noise.

11
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:35.433
Hand picking means they may be exactly where the new prompt shines.

12
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.833
The grader is invested in the outcome.

13
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:42.427
And with two variables changed, the comparison can't be interpreted.

14
00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:45.900
Arguing about prompt wording is only taste, so skip it.

15
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.833
Now, how you say it matters as much as being right.

16
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.865
Don't open with the weakness.

17
00:00:51.865 --> 00:00:54.133
Open with what is genuinely promising.

18
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:58.267
Then propose running it on the golden set, so you help prove their idea.

19
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.533
The golden set becomes the arbiter, not your opinion.

20
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:03.433
Lead with belief, not doubt.

21
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:06.467
So what would make you happy to ship it?

22
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:10.167
The same two hundred example golden set for both prompts.

23
00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:13.400
The same temperature, so only one thing changes.

24
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:19.900
Blind grading, with an LLM judge that swaps answer order, or a colleague who wrote neither prompt.

25
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.873
And a per item diff showing what improved and what regressed.

26
00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:25.800
If the gain survives, it's real.

27
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.767
The trap is being right and alienating everyone.

28
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:33.567
Frame it as a shared goal: a number we can defend to leadership.

29
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:35.539
Let's recap.

30
00:01:35.539 --> 00:01:39.467
Twelve hand picked, author graded examples are weak evidence.

31
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.167
Skip taste debates and fix the evidence.

32
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.900
Lead with belief, then bring the golden set.

33
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.560
Ship on blind, same settings results.

34
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.367
Next up: the model that got worse overnight.
